This project's decoders are transcriptions/ports of algorithms from:

  * XADMaster — the engine of The Unarchiver, by Dag Ågren (MacPaw).
    https://github.com/MacPaw/XADMaster — LGPL-2.1.
    StuffIt (classic + SIT5), Compact Pro, PackIt and related handles
    were used as the primary reference.
    NOT BinHex, MacBinary, AppleSingle/AppleDouble, zip, tar or gzip:
    since 2026-09-19 each of those container parsers cites the public
    document its facts are written in instead (the "CONTAINER FORMAT
    SPECIFICATIONS" section below). Four things that no public
    document supports are marked as XADMaster's at the line that makes
    them, and stay — the MacBinary CRC-16/0xA001 fallback for files
    written with the wrong table, the nonzero-date test that stands in
    for a MacBinary I header's missing checksum, the byte-swapped
    AppleSingle header, and the whole StuffIt split header in
    `src/fmt_sitseg.c`, which Aladdin never published and which nothing
    else in `reference/` reads (deark and macutils were both looked
    at). Of the three detect-only probes in `src/fmt_stubs.c`, **none
    is XADMaster's any longer**: StuffIt X's magic is deark's,
    DiskDoubler's two older headers are in macutils' `macunpack/dd.h`
    — magic, offsets, CRC position and all — and since 2026-09-21 the
    Now Compress probe cites `docs/formats/now-compress.md`, where the
    file header, the entry record and the byte-sum checksum it tests
    are measured off nine archives.
    Arsenic (StuffIt method 15) no longer: since 2026-09-19
    `src/sit_arsenic.c` is written from Matthew T. Russotto's public
    description of the format (reference/notes/arsenic_russotto.md),
    with a move-to-front in the two-level shape of bzip2's (Julian
    Seward, BSD-style licence), written from the idea. Nor LZH's
    static methods: since 2026-09-19 `src/fmt_lzh.c`'s decoders for
    -lh4- .. -lh7- and for LArc's -lz5-/-lzs- are written from lhasa
    (below); XADLZHParser.m remains the source of that file's header
    walk and CRC contract. Nor the LZW decoder: since 2026-09-19
    `src/unc_lzw.c` is written from deark's `src/fmtutil-lzw.c` (below)
    and Welch's algorithm, and neither `LZW.c` nor
    `XADCompressHandle.m` is a source of it. Nor the canonical
    code-length Huffman codes in `src/unc_huffman.c`, which follow
    RFC 1951 section 3.2.2 and puff.c (below) rather than
    XADPrefixCode.m. Nor, since 2026-09-20, the bit-serialized TREE in
    that same file, nor the fact that DiskDoubler method 4 and PackIt
    write the same one: those are
    `docs/formats/serialized-huffman-tree.md`, this project's own
    description, derived from the three formats' output by experiment
    and reproducible with `tools/derive_huffman_tree.py`. StuffIt
    method 8 went the same way that day —
    `docs/formats/stuffit-method-8-mw.md`, Miller and Wegman's
    dictionary rule with the parameters measured off all four method-8
    forks in the corpus (`tools/derive_stuffit8.py`) — **except for
    three rules no specimen reaches**: the table restart, the
    end-of-stream code and the 14-bit ceiling, which are still
    XADStuffItOldHandles.m's and say so where they are used. And ARC's
    member header, its RLE90 filter and its methods 4, 8 and 9 are
    `docs/formats/arc-container-and-coders.md` since the same day,
    derived from archives ARC 5.21q wrote to order
    (`tools/derive_arc.py`); XADARCCrunchHandle.m still has methods
    5/6/7 and XADARCCrushHandle.m method 10. And **Compact Pro** is
    `docs/formats/compact-pro.md` since the same day — the format's
    own published description, three pages of The Unarchiver's format
    wiki, plus the six corrections it needs; a reader written from
    those alone reads every Compact Pro fork in the corpus, 536 of
    them (`tools/derive_cpt.py`), so neither XADCompactProParser.m nor
    XADCompactProLZHHandle.m nor macutils' cpt.c is a source of
    `src/fmt_cpt.c` any longer. Nor of `src/fmt_sit.c`'s container,
    its method 13 or `src/sit13_tables.h`: that wiki documents method
    13 in full, tables included, and
    `docs/formats/stuffit-method-13.md` carries the two corrections it
    needs — 897 forks across 103 archives read from the page alone
    (`tools/derive_sit13.py`), and the tables compared array by array
    against XADStuffIt13Handle.m's before `gen_sit13_tables.py` was
    pointed at the page instead. What that file still owes XADMaster
    and macutils is **method 6**, and that is the whole of it since
    2026-09-21: the RLE90 filter is
    `reference/notes/rle90_theunarchiver.md`, the format's own
    published description, which states every rule
    `unc_internal.h`'s shared filter implements — including the
    `0x90 0x01` case it calls open and this project settled by
    measurement. And **DiskDoubler's
    coders** since 2026-09-21: the same wiki documents methods 6 and 9
    (`ADn`) and method 10 (`DDn`), and
    `docs/formats/diskdoubler-dd.md` carries the six corrections they
    need — including method 10's second form, which the page names and
    does not describe — so a reader written from those pages alone
    reads all 39 DD and AD forks in the corpus
    (`tools/derive_dd.py`). `src/fmt_dd.c`'s CONTAINERS are still
    XADDiskDoublerParser.m's, which is also what says which forks use
    that second form; the wiki punts DiskDoubler's containers to that
    file by name.
    **Now Compress is a different case.** The FORMAT is described by
    this project — `docs/formats/now-compress.md`, measured off Now
    Compress 5.0.1 under emulation, with a reader written from that
    note alone decoding every block of both corpus archives
    (`tools/derive_now.py`) — because no published description of it
    exists anywhere. Its coders in `src/fmt_now.c` were **rewritten
    from that note on 2026-09-21**, the M12 transcription of
    XADNowCompressParser.m and XADNowCompressHandle.m having stood
    until then. **Nothing in that file rests on the reference any
    more**: the last two things that did — the offset-delta search and
    the code-length escape past 15, neither of which the encoder will
    produce on request — were measured against the format's own decoder
    the same day, by changing the field and handing the file back to
    Now Compress (`tools/mutate_now.py`).
    Files that transcribe XADMaster code carry a
    "Source: reference/XADMaster/<file>" comment.

  * macutils 2.0b3, by Dik T. Winter et al. (descended from Allan G.
    Weber's unsit and D. J. Perry's unstuffit) — late-80s C. Its README
    states that every algorithm in it is implemented from publicly
    available software, that use and redistribution are explicitly
    allowed, and that "for some parts of it the GNU software license
    does apply"; the files here that cite it say "LGPL, see NOTICE",
    and this is what that points at.
    Used as a secondary reference for the oldest formats, and as the
    ONLY reference for StuffIt method 6, "fixed Huffman", which
    XADMaster recognizes but does not implement: `src/fmt_sit.c` and
    the generated `src/sit6_tables.h` transcribe `macunpack/sit.c`.
    **Not `src/fmt_packit.c`'s container since 2026-09-21**: PackIt is
    published too, in appendices B and C of R. Scott McGinnis's 1986
    unPACKIT manual, and `docs/formats/packit.md` carries the four
    corrections they need — a reader written from those appendices
    alone reads all 38 forks of the corpus's PackIt archives
    (`tools/derive_packit.py`). What that file still owes macutils is
    that the two CRCs the appendix names are CRC-16/XMODEM and the
    resync strategy for an entry it cannot decode; the two ciphers are
    XADMaster's.
    A build of `macunpack` is also the second oracle for the
    differential test suite (TESTING.md) — test-time only, not linked
    into anything shipped.

  * LZHUF, by Haruyasu Yoshizaki (1988) with comments by Haruhiko
    Okumura (1989) — the published program, LZHUF.C, as distributed in
    http://cd.textfiles.com/rbbsv3n1/pac4/okumura.zip. It carries no
    copyright notice and no licence text; its terms of use are
    reconstructed (Russell Marks, 2001, via deark's readme-lzhuf.txt)
    as "use, distribute, and modify this program freely". Since
    2026-09-19 `src/unc_lzah.c` — the -lh1- coder shared by StuffIt
    method 5, LZH -lh1-, ARC 7.x Trimmed and DART's "best" — follows
    that program's tree machinery (freq/son/prnt, reconst, update,
    the 256-entry distance lookup derived from the 64 code lengths)
    rather than XADMaster's transcription of it; the modes, the window
    preloads and everything around the tree are this project's. The
    change was made for speed and heap, not licence: this engine
    remains LGPL through its other XADMaster transcriptions.

  * deark, by Jason Summers — https://github.com/jsummers/deark, MIT.
    Two ARC methods in `src/fmt_arc.c` follow deark rather than
    XADMaster: 0x0b ("Distilled"), whose reading comes from deark's
    `src/fmtutil-lzh.c`, and 0x0a as ARC 7.x means it ("Trimmed"),
    whose parameters — a stop code, match lengths 3..59 and a
    zero-filled window — come from `foreign/lzhuf.h`'s
    `is_arc_trimmed` branch, with the dialect test that tells Trimmed
    from PAK's Crushed taken from `modules/arc.c`. The first is
    followed instead of XADMaster's because XADMaster's is wrong
    about one thing and the CRC PAK stored says so: the 8 KB window
    starts full of spaces (BLOCKERS.md). No code was copied — the file
    states the facts it took and cites them — and deark's own
    documentation of the format (entropymine.wordpress.com,
    "ARC/PAK's Distilled compression scheme") is cited alongside it.
    Since 2026-09-19 deark is also the source of `src/unc_lzw.c`, the
    Unix compress(1) LZW decoder: `src/fmtutil-lzw.c` documents the
    stream — the clear code, where the code width grows, and the rule
    that codes are written eight at a time at one width, so a width
    change mid-group leaves padding to discard. The algorithm is
    Welch's. Again no code was copied, and `LZW.c` and
    `XADCompressHandle.m` are no longer sources of that file. The
    same file's `delzw_process_unixcompress_3byteheader` is what
    `src/fmt_exo.c` cites for the `.Z` header the decoder sits behind
    — the magic, the maximum code width in the mode byte's low five
    bits, and bit 7 for the clear code.
    A build of deark is also the oracle for that method in the test
    suite (TESTING.md, tools/build_deark.sh): test-time only, never
    linked into anything shipped.
    Since 2026-09-19 it is also what `unc_sitx_probe` in
    `src/fmt_stubs.c` cites for the StuffIt X magic — an eight-byte
    signature Aladdin never published, which
    `modules/unsupported.c` reads exactly as this does, so the probe
    no longer needs XADMaster's reading of it.

  * mpack/munpack 1.6, by John G. Myers — Copyright (c) 1993, 1994
    Carnegie Mellon University. Permissive: "Permission to use, copy,
    modify, distribute, and sell this software and its documentation
    for any purpose is hereby granted without fee, provided that the
    above copyright notice appear in all copies and that both that
    copyright notice and this permission notice appear in supporting
